    translation: "After a knee operation Stefan was told that further physiotherapy was ‘of limited value’, a wording he first heard as a polite goodbye. His clinician meant that after twelve weeks the guideline no longer supports standard reimbursement, except in the case of demonstrable complications. Stefan asked for a second opinion, not out of distrust of the surgeon, but because he noticed that climbing stairs at home felt different from the tests in the treatment room. The second hospital confirmed the operation, but criticised the pace of the build-up. The letter said pain relief had masked the tests, which the first practice had not recorded. That sentence was cautious enough not to feed a disciplinary complaint and sharp enough to justify an extension of therapy. The health insurer initially refused, referring to its own purchasing policy. Stefan sent both letters plus a diary of functional limitations, written without drama. Only when a medical adviser admitted by phone that ‘of limited value’ is not a synonym for ‘medically pointless’ did an authorisation for six extra weeks come through. Stefan kept the correspondence as a lesson in register: anyone who wants reimbursement must cite the guideline and at the same time make daily life concrete. The operation took place in a hospital in Nieuwegein. The first practice stopped after twelve weeks, because after that the guideline no longer supports standard reimbursement. A second hospital wrote that pain relief had masked the walk test, without presenting dosages as an official decision. A health insurer initially refused an authorisation, referring to its own purchasing policy. Stefan’s diary still counted in week thirteen fourteen moments when the stairs to the second floor took too long. After that the authorisation for six extra weeks came through. In the objection email he cited the guideline on functional stagnation, not his irritation at the tone.",
