    translation: "At the university of applied sciences a petition circulated against the binding study advice in the first year, after a cohort had missed practical modules because of the long-term illness of two lecturers. The examination board maintained that the standard was not arbitrary, though it acknowledged that catch-up teaching had been organised too late. Students did not demand automatic passes, but a recalculation of the credit total without the cancelled practicals. In media reports the conflict quickly became a debate about a ‘snowflake generation’. Anyone who read the documents saw something narrower: a procedure that assumes a fully delivered programme, applied to a year that had not had that programme. The board feared a precedent; the students feared that leniency would only be distributed informally, and thus unequally. In the end there was an addendum: anyone who had demonstrably missed modules could take two resits outside the BSA window, without grades being raised. Nobody called it a victory. The student assessor wrote in the report that the tone of the petition had been sharper than the negotiation, and that precisely that sharpness had forced the council to record the exception in writing. The university of applied sciences counted a first-year cohort that had missed practical modules because of the illness of two lecturers. The examination board kept the BSA standard in place, while dozens of students had demonstrably missed practical credits. The petition gathered hundreds of signatures, some of them from outside the programme, which gave the media the snowflake frame. The director promised in writing that resits fall outside the window, without grades being raised, and that the leniency applies only with a statement from the team leader. The student assessor put that in the report, so that later nobody can say that only atmosphere was promised. The exception now stands in black and white, not only in the corridors.",
